+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new service for larder, such as a store, a queue or a search engine, or reviews such a
+proposal. It gives the tenets the team weighs a proposal against. Written after the same argument was had three times
+from the start: Redis for sessions in 2023, Redis for Celery's queue, which evicted 300 receipt jobs in March 2025,
+and Elasticsearch for pantry item search in issue 70, which Postgres full-text search answered instead.
+
+## Tenets
+
+- **Larder runs one database and one web app, and a new service must do something Postgres cannot, with two
+  volunteers able to restore it.** The volunteers who run larder are few, and a service only one of them can restore
+  is down whenever that one is away.
+  - Rules out: a service added because it is faster or more common for a job Postgres can do, such as Redis for
+    sessions or a queue, or Elasticsearch for searching item names; and any service only one volunteer knows how to
+    restore.
+  - Does not rule out: a service outside larder that no volunteer runs, such as the card gateway or a hosted service.
+  - Applied in [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+    [run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
